I read the Toolathlon task source, the task/evaluation files, and the `gemini-3-pro-preview_1.jsonl` trajectory row for `email-paper-homepage`.

## Task setup and expected work

The task given to the agent was:

> “Update my personal homepage according to the following rules:
> - For papers currently marked as ‘preprint’ or ‘under review’ on my homepage, update their acceptance information according to my emails.
> - Update the status of code open-sourcing for these accepted papers only. If there is a released repository on my GitHub for the corresponding paper, update it on my homepage.”

The broader task README describes a publication-update workflow using two MCP systems:

- email tools to find acceptance/camera-ready/congratulations emails;
- GitHub tools to update the `My-Homepage` repository directly;
- publication files live under `_publications`;
- venue/status should be updated only when supported by email evidence;
- `codeurl` should be added only when a corresponding GitHub repository is actually released/finished;
- unreleased or still-under-review repositories should not be linked.

The evaluation script checks, among other things, that:

- `_publications/2025-06-01-ipsum-lorem-all-you-need.md` contains `COML 2025`;
- `_publications/2025-06-15-ipsum-lorem-workshop.md` contains `COMLW 2025`;
- `_publications/2025-07-01-optimizing-llms-contextual-reasoning.md` contains `COAI 2025`;
- released-paper `codeurl`s should exist for:
  - `enhancing-llms`;
  - `ipsum-lorem-all-you-need`;
  - `llm-adaptive-learning`;
- the `optimizing-llms-contextual-reasoning` paper should **not** have a `codeurl`, because its repository README says it is “🚧 To be released.”

The trajectory row records:

- model/run: `gemini-3-pro-preview_1`;
- task: `email-paper-homepage`;
- running status: `done`;
- evaluation: `false`;
- 34 tool calls;
- 31 agent LLM requests.

## Chronological account of the agent’s run

### 1. Initial attempt to access a presumed homepage repository

The agent first tried to read a GitHub repository it guessed as:

```json
github-get_file_contents {
  "repo": "homepage",
  "owner": "my-personal-website"
}
```

The tool returned:

```text
missing required parameter: path
```

So the first GitHub call did not access any repository content.

### 2. Attempted to list repositories with a non-existent tool

The agent then tried:

```json
github-list_repositories {
  "query": "user:me"
}
```

The tool response was:

```text
Tool github-list_repositories not found in agent Assistant
```

It later tried the same unavailable tool again with owner/repo-style arguments and received the same “tool not found” response.

### 3. Tried searching for a guessed user/repository

The agent next used the available repository search tool:

```json
github-search_repositories {
  "query": "user:test-user homepage"
}
```

GitHub returned a 422 validation failure saying the listed users/repositories could not be searched, either because they did not exist or permission was unavailable.

### 4. Retrieved the authenticated GitHub user

The agent called:

```json
github-get_me {}
```

The tool returned the authenticated user:

```json
{
  "login": "toolathlon-testuser",
  "profile_url": "https://github.com/toolathlon-testuser",
  "details": {
    "public_repos": 8,
    ...
  }
}
```

This gave the agent the actual GitHub owner name to use.

### 5. Searched the authenticated user’s repositories

The agent searched:

```json
github-search_repositories {
  "query": "user:toolathlon-testuser"
}
```

The tool returned six repositories, including:

- `toolathlon-testuser/My-Homepage`, default branch `master`;
- `toolathlon-testuser/enhancing-llms`;
- `toolathlon-testuser/llm-adaptive-learning`;
- `toolathlon-testuser/optimizing-llms-contextual-reasoning`;
- `toolathlon-testuser/ipsum-lorem-all-you-need`;
- `toolathlon-testuser/LUFFY`.

